# The audio device's entry in the launcher's app table, merged into the launcher
# config `RobotCpu` renders (`cogs/robot.clk`) so that one supervisor starts and
# stops every process a unit runs.
#
# One message of simplelaunch's `AppConfig`. Hand-written for the reason
# `driver/motord_launch.textproto` and `host/host_launch.textproto` are: the
# renderer only knows about Clockwork processes, and the pod is not one -- it is
# brenn-pod's binary, holding the mic array over USB and the board's ALSA
# devices, and it reaches this repo as a prebuilt artifact rather than as a
# target of this build.
#
# The executable is resolved against the launcher's working directory, which is
# the payload root -- so this is where `tools/build-motion.sh` stages the pod
# binary. The argument selects the streaming pipeline; the same binary also
# carries self-test registries.
#
# No configuration argument, because the pod takes none: it reads
# `/run/brenn-app/conf/audio.conf` by a compiled-in path, which is a per-unit
# push into RAM because it holds that unit's link key. A pod started before that
# file is there parks and re-reads rather than exiting, so this app is safe to
# start on a unit whose credentials have not been pushed yet -- what it does then
# is wait, in its own console log.
#
# Named `pod` because that is this repo's word for the audio device, and the
# launcher spells each app's console `<name>_<run>.log`.
#
# Merged into the production config only. The harness twin
# (`cogs:robotcpu_harness.textproto`) leaves it out for the same reason it leaves
# the voice host out: a motion run must need no audio hardware, no speech
# services and nothing streaming off the board.
app {
  name: "pod"
  executable: "reachy_pod"
  args: "run"
}
